    "justification": "Canonicality has operational value only if conditional shared-cell covariance and its loading can be learned without a first-order penalty.",
    "gap": "Akbari, Kiyavash, and Ghassami (2025, Theorem 5) derive the panel DDD efficient influence function, while their Theorem 7 treats repeated cross-sections and Chernozhukov et al. (2018, Theorem 3.1) give generic DML; none controls a learned all-cohort panel-DDD covariance loading.",
    "consumer": "The theorem supplies the statistical contract for an optional efficient backend in the CRAN package \\(\\texttt{triplediff}\\).",
    "proof_tex": "Write \\(\\eta=(p,m)\\), and let \\(R(\\eta,\\pi)\\) denote the raw ratio pseudo-outcome.  Conditional on each training sample, cross-fitting makes the validation observations independent of \\(\\widehat\\eta^{-\\ell}\\), \\(\\widehat\\Gamma^{-\\ell}\\), and \\(\\widehat b^{-\\ell}\\).  Feasibility gives \\(A'(\\widehat b^{-\\ell}-b^\\star)=0\\) exactly.  Because the oracle conditional mean of every raw score in target block \\(k\\) is the same, namely \\(p_{g,1}(X)q_k(X)/\\pi_{g,1}\\),\n\\[\nE_P[(\\widehat b^{-\\ell}(X)-b^\\star(X))'R_P(O)\\mid X,\\text{training}]=0.\n\\]\n\nFor each cell term, add and subtract the true regression inside the estimated inverse-propensity residual.  The conditional expectation of the first-order propensity error and the first-order regression error vanishes, leaving a product.  Finite \\(M,T\\), overlap, and Hölder's inequality consequently give the foldwise bias bound\n\\[\n\\left|P\\{b^{\\star\\prime}(R(\\widehat\\eta,\\pi)-R(\\eta,\\pi))\\}\\right|\\le C r_{p,n}r_{m,n},\n\\]\nand, after inserting \\(\\widehat b-b^\\star\\), the conservative mixed bound\n\\[\n\\left|P\\{(\\widehat b-b^\\star)'(R(\\widehat\\eta,\\pi)-R(\\eta,\\pi))\\}\\right|\n\\le C r_{b,n}(r_{p,n}+r_{m,n}).\n\\]\nBoth are \\(o_P(n^{-1/2})\\) by the two product-rate assumptions.  The fourth-moment envelope, nuisance consistency, and the loading perturbation lemma also imply\n\\[\n\\|\\widehat b'R(\\widehat\\eta,\\pi)-b^{\\star\\prime}R(\\eta,\\pi)\\|_{P,2}=o_P(1).\n\\]\nConditional Chebyshev inequality then makes the corresponding centered validation-fold empirical process \\(o_P(n^{-1/2})\\).\n\nIt remains to expand the empirical target-cell shares.  Since \\(\\pi_{g,1}\\ge\\varepsilon\\),\n\\[\n\\widehat\\pi_{g,1}^{-1}-\\pi_{g,1}^{-1}\n=-\\pi_{g,1}^{-2}(\\widehat\\pi_{g,1}-\\pi_{g,1})+O_P(n^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe mean numerator in every coordinate of target block \\(k\\) equals \\(\\pi_{g,1}\\theta_k\\), and feasibility sums its derivative with coefficient \\(\\omega_k\\).  Thus the denominator expansion contributes\n\\[\n-\\sum_{k=(g,t)}\\omega_k\\frac{\\theta_k}{\\pi_{g,1}}\\,n^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\{\\mathbf 1_{g,1,i}-\\pi_{g,1}\\}.\n\\]\nCombining this term with the raw-score empirical term gives exactly the corrected score \\(b^\\star(X)'\\Psi_P(O)\\).  Therefore\n\\[\n\\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_{\\mathrm{eff}}-\\tau_\\omega)\n=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{i=1}^n b^\\star(X_i)'\\Psi_P(O_i)+o_P(1).\n\\]\nThe corrected estimated influence values consistently include the displayed denominator term.  Their \\(L_2(P)\\) convergence and the conditional weak law give \\(\\widehat V_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\to_PV_\\star\\).  The cited i.i.d. central limit theorem, Slutsky's theorem, \\(V_\\star>0\\), and the definition of the normal quantile yield the stated pointwise limit and coverage.  The canonical-gradient correction above establishes that this is score-family attainment, and identifies the additional tangent-exhaustion condition required for a semiparametric-efficiency interpretation.",
